    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay i get the pipe thing. so if the LDL is the gunk, how does it actually get stuck there? like what's physically happening to the artery walls when that happens?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The process of LDL getting stuck in your artery walls is a bit like a slow, messy invasion, which is a process called atherosclerosis. When LDL cholesterol is too high, it can penetrate the delicate inner lining of your arteries. These particles get trapped, and your body tries to fight them off, which causes inflammation. Over time, this trapped LDL gets oxidized, and immune cells gather around it, forming fatty streaks that eventually harden and narrow the artery. This buildup is what restricts blood flow, making the arteries less flexible and more prone to dangerous blockages.",
      "source": "target",
